Member Type Presence Notes <code>op</code> String REQUIRED One of the fifteen values below. <code>operand</code> Node REQUIRED The operand. <code>op</code> Category Operand type Result Semantics <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>abs</code> Numeric Numeric Numeric Operators §4.2 <code>sqrt</code>, <code>exp</code>, <code>ln</code> Numeric Numeric Number Operators §4.2 <code>isnull</code> Comparison any Boolean Operators §5.10 <code>not</code> Logical Boolean Boolean Operators §6 <code>len</code> String String Integer Operators §9.1 <code>year</code>, <code>semester</code>, <code>quarter</code>, <code>month</code>, <code>week</code>, <code>day</code> Time Date Integer Operators §10.2 <p>The six date-component extractors share one signature and differ only in the component returned (Operators §10.2.1), so they take one <code>op</code> value each on the same Node shape. <code>sqrt</code>, <code>exp</code> and <code>ln</code> are likewise unary numeric functions, alongside <code>abs</code>.</p> <p>Note that <code>sqrt</code>, <code>exp</code> and <code>ln</code> always yield Number, never Integer, even for an Integer operand (Operators §4.1); and that they reject Number Interval operands, so a Consumer MUST NOT apply interval arithmetics to them even where the operand <code>VarID</code> carries <code>interval: true</code>.</p> <p>A date-component extractor takes any Date-valued Node. The commonest is <code>[get refPeriod]</code>, the reference period of the instance read as a Component (§4.23), since a reported Date Variable is comparatively rare.</p> <pre><code>{ "class_name": "UnaryOp", "op": "isnull",
  "operand": { "class_name": "VarID", … } }
</code></pre>
